{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And it shall be for a sign upon your hand, etc.</b> Our Sages of blessed memory (Menachot 37a) said: ״יָדְכָה״ — יָד כֵּהָה, which is the left hand. And one needs to know why HaShem did not choose the right hand for the performance of the commandment, since it is the more distinguished. And our Sages said (there) that it is because it is positioned opposite the heart, which is on the left side — and their words are true. Yet it appears to me to give a good reason, for the verse itself gave the reason for the matter by writing <b>כִּי בְּחֹזֶק יָד</b>, for you must know that the Master blessed be He has two aspects in His attributes: one is called the great hand (יָד הַגְּדוֹלָה), and one is called the strong hand (יָד הַחֲזָקָה). The great hand is the side of lovingkindness and good, and the strong hand is the might that repays the doer of evil according to his evil. Now, in bringing Israel out of Egypt, HaShem inclined the strong hand and struck His enemies with ten plagues; and therefore the Master decreed that the laying of tefillin, with its inscription of remembrance, be upon our weaker hand (יָד כֵּהָה), for it is a representation of the strong hand that brought us out of Egypt. And this is what the verse says here: <b>כִּי בְּחֹזֶק יָד</b> — and it is correct. And although we find that He said in many places ״יָד הַגְּדוֹלָה״, that is the acquiescence of the attribute of mercy to the attribute of judgment, but the principal mode is called the strong hand." "<b>Thus is concluded the section Bo</b>"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:27:31.754726795Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}